    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1165-a6">

                <pb facs="1248" n="1152" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg1152"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Second indorsement.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bureau of Medicine and Surgery,<lb/>Department of the
                            Navy</hi></placeName>,<lb/><date calendar="gregorian" when="1898-05-06"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">May 6, 1898</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 40876.]</seg></opener>
                <p>Subject: State Department, May 4, 1898, modus vivendi, ambulance ship <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Solace</hi>, General Orders, No. 487.</p>
                <p>Noted, and respectfully returned to Department.</p>
                <pb facs="1249" n="1153" xml:id="pg1153"/>
                <p>As the ambulance ship <hi rendition="#italic">Solace</hi> has been fitted and
                    equipped under the terms of the Geneva Convention, it is earnestly requested
                    that the Department recommend the recognition and carrying into execution as a
                    modus vivendi during the duration of hostiltiies the additional articles
                    referred to.</p>
                <p>The <hi rendition="#italic">Solace</hi> is the first government vessel of any
                    nation fitted and equipped under these terms, and it is due to the United States
                    that her status should receive international recognition.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. K. Van Reypen</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Surgeon-General, United States Navy.</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
